<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.1" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="42349-1" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Reason for referral (narrative)"/>
    <title>Reason for Referral</title>
    <text><paragraph>Albumin in the urine and mildly reduced kidney function in a 49-year-old woman with newly diagnosed type 2 diabetes, high blood pressure readings awaiting home confirmation, and kidneys injured in a beating years ago. Please advise on the cause, the need for imaging, kidney-protective treatment, and monitoring. A telehealth visit from our health center is requested, because the trip to the regional hospital is long and she has no car. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Urgency: Within two weeks, by telehealth on the day of her two-week visit, with our community health worker present. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Questions for the consultant:</paragraph><list listType="ordered"><item>What is the likely cause of the albuminuria and reduced eGFR: her old kidney injury, diabetes, high blood pressure, or a combination?</item><item>Is a kidney ultrasound needed, and does anything else need to be excluded?</item><item>If hypertension is confirmed, which blood pressure medicines do you advise, and what is her target?</item><item>Should she start an SGLT2 inhibitor now, and is there a role for finerenone?</item><item>Is metformin safe at this kidney function, and what should she avoid?</item><item>How often should kidney function, potassium and urine albumin be checked, given how hard travel is for her?</item></list></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="1.3.6.1.4.1.19376.1.5.3.1.3.4"/>
    <code code="10164-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="History of Present illness Narrative"/>
    <title>History of Present Illness</title>
    <text><paragraph>Mrs. Hamer, 49, is seen by video from the community health center at the request of Dr. Batiste-Hill, with the community health worker beside her and her consent to the telehealth visit. She describes injuries to her kidneys and left eye from a beating years ago, and back and leg aching since. She has had thirst and frequent urination for some months, easing on metformin. Her home blood pressure readings over seven days average 152/94. She takes no NSAIDs. She has no blood in the urine, no kidney stones and no known family kidney disease. She said she would agree to treatment if each medicine and its purpose were explained, and asked what the medicines would cost. [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Historical basis. These claims are historical evidence, not findings from a modern examination.</paragraph><list><item>Fannie Lou Hamer was born on October 6, 1917, in Montgomery County, Mississippi, the twentieth child of Lou Ella and James Lee Townsend, sharecroppers. She joined her family in the cotton fields at six and left school after the sixth grade, at 12, to work on the cotton plantations of the Delta. [D]</item><item>She worked for eighteen years as a sharecropper and timekeeper on a Delta plantation, made timekeeper because she could read and write. In 1964 she gave her home as Ruleville, in Sunflower County, Mississippi. [D]</item><item>In 1961, hospitalized for abdominal pain, she was found to have a uterine tumor; during the operation to remove it, a white surgeon performed a hysterectomy without her knowledge or informed consent. She later called this a &quot;Mississippi appendectomy,&quot; a name for the sterilizations done without consent to Black women in the South. [D]</item><item>On August 31, 1962, she tried to register to vote in Indianola; when she refused to withdraw her application, the plantation owner ordered her off his land and she had to leave that same night. [D]</item><item>On June 9, 1963, returning from a voter-registration workshop, she was arrested with other activists in Winona, Mississippi, and in the jail two Black prisoners were ordered by officers to beat her with a blackjack, as she testified the next year. [D]</item><item>The beating left her with lifelong injuries to her eyes, legs and kidneys: permanent kidney damage, and a blood clot in her left eye with loss of vision in one eye. [D]</item><item>On August 22, 1964, she testified before the Credentials Committee of the Democratic National Convention in Atlantic City about her eviction and the Winona beating. [D]</item></list><paragraph>2 later historical claims are omitted because they describe events after the index age. See the teaching note.</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

