<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.8"/>
    <code code="51848-0" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation note"/>
    <title>Assessment</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item>Chronic kidney disease G2 A2 (N18.2). The small, scarred right kidney is a structural abnormality consistent with her old injury and is in itself chronic kidney disease; with an eGFR of 73 and albuminuria confirmed on two samples, she is G2 A2. Diabetes and high blood pressure probably add to the albumin loss. Nothing on ultrasound suggests blockage, stones or another cause, and no further imaging is needed. [S]</item><item>Hypertension with chronic kidney disease (I12.9). Stage 2 hypertension is confirmed by home readings. With albuminuria an ACE inhibitor is the foundation, and at this level two medicines together are recommended; a single combined capsule keeps cost and pill count down. Target below 130/80. [S]</item><item>Type 2 diabetes with chronic kidney disease (E11.22). An SGLT2 inhibitor protects the kidneys and heart at her eGFR and albuminuria, independent of its glucose effect. Metformin is safe at this eGFR at full dose. Finerenone is for albuminuria that persists despite an ACE inhibitor at the dose she tolerates and an SGLT2 inhibitor, and can wait. [S]</item></list><paragraph>Data reviewed: Dr. Batiste-Hill&apos;s intake note and results: creatinine 0.96 mg/dL, eGFR 73; potassium 4.3 mmol/L; urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio 88 mg/g at intake and 96 mg/g this morning; A1c 8.6%; LDL 146 mg/dL; ECG without left ventricular hypertrophy. Kidney ultrasound this morning: right kidney 9.0 cm with a lower-pole cortical scar, left kidney 11.4 cm and normal, no hydronephrosis or stones. Home blood pressure average 152/94; clinic 156/96 today. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.10" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="18776-5" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Plan of care note"/>
    <title>Plan of Treatment</title>
    <text><list><item>Amlodipine 5 mg with benazepril 20 mg as one daily capsule; blood pressure goal below 130/80, with home readings. [S]</item><item>Dapagliflozin 10 mg daily, at the health center&apos;s discount pharmacy price; stop it, with benazepril, on days of vomiting, diarrhea or not eating, and restart when eating and drinking again. [S]</item><item>Potassium and creatinine one to two weeks after starting, accepting a creatinine rise of up to 30%; then every six months, scheduled on days she is already at the health center. [S]</item><item>Urine albumin-to-creatinine ratio again in three to four months, then every six months; aim for at least a 30% fall. [S]</item><item>Continue metformin and atorvastatin; avoid ibuprofen, naproxen and other NSAIDs, and tell any clinician about her kidneys before contrast dye. [S]</item><item>No further imaging; repeat the ultrasound only if kidney function falls unexpectedly. [S]</item><item>If albumin stays moderately increased after six months on both medicines, review by telehealth about increasing benazepril or adding finerenone. [S]</item><item>Discussed with Dr. Batiste-Hill by phone after the video visit. Each medicine, its purpose, its cost and its side effects were explained to Mrs. Hamer, who asked questions and agreed to start both; she was told she could stop either at any time and should call first if she wanted to. [S]</item><item>Follow-up: Telehealth review after six months of treatment if albumin remains raised, or sooner for a fall in eGFR of more than 30%, potassium above 5.5 mmol/L, or blood pressure not at goal on two medicines; Dr. Batiste-Hill continues care and monitoring. [S]</item></list></text>

  </section>
